{"book":"PSA","chapter":114,"source":"hbo","verses":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8],"records":[{"id":"PSA.114.1.s1","verses":[1],"source":"בְּצֵ֣את יִ֭שְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרָ֑יִם בֵּ֥ית יַ֝עֲקֹ֗ב מֵעַ֥ם לֹעֵֽז׃","gloss":"When Israel went out from Egypt, [the] house of Jacob from a people of strange speech,","pieces":[[1,"When Israel left Egypt, when the house of Jacob left a people whose speech was strange to them,",null]],"L":{"1":"When Israel went out from Egypt, the house of Jacob from a people of strange speech,"},"R":{"1":"Long ago the people of Israel, the family of Jacob, left Egypt, a land where people spoke a language they did not know."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"allusion","tokens":["o191140010012","o191140010021","o191140010032"],"choice":"the exodus from Egypt","alternatives":[],"reason":"The psalm retells the going out from Egypt (Exodus 12-14), the crossing of the sea and of the Jordan (Exodus 14; Joshua 3), the trembling of Sinai (Exodus 19:18) and the water from the rock (Exodus 17:6). It is sung at Passover.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191140010071"],"choice":"strange speech: speaking a language not understood","alternatives":[],"reason":"The verb (la'az) is used of speaking unintelligibly. Egypt was a foreign land whose language Israel did not share (81:6).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o191140010021"],"choice":"a psalm that holds back God's name","alternatives":[],"reason":"The psalm speaks of \"his\" sanctuary (verse 2) and asks the sea and mountains what they saw, but names the Lord only in verse 7. The ancient Greek translation joins Psalms 114 and 115 as one psalm.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m4","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140010071"],"choice":"a sentence that runs on","alternatives":[],"reason":"The sentence continues into verse 2, so this verse ends with a comma at Literal and Balanced.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"supplied_words","tokens":["o191140010011"],"choice":"Long ago","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable marks the event as past history.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":"When Israel came out of Egypt","status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.2.s1","verses":[2],"source":"הָיְתָ֣ה יְהוּדָ֣ה לְקָדְשׁ֑וֹ יִ֝שְׂרָאֵ֗ל מַמְשְׁלוֹתָֽיו׃","gloss":"Judah became his sanctuary, Israel his dominion.","pieces":[[2,"Judah became {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} holy place, Israel the realm {{deity_pronoun:he|He}} rules.",null]],"L":{"2":"Judah became {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} sanctuary, Israel {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} dominion."},"R":{"2":"God made Judah {{deity_pronoun:his|His}} holy home, and Israel became the kingdom {{deity_pronoun:he|He}} rules."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140020033","o191140020052"],"choice":"his: God's, though not yet named","alternatives":[],"reason":"\"His\" can only be God, whom the psalm names only in verse 7 (m3 at 114:1).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191140020032"],"choice":"sanctuary: a holy place, or holy possession","alternatives":["his holy people"],"reason":"The word (qodesh) is holiness, or a holy place. The people itself became the place of God's presence and his own possession (Exodus 19:6).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"idiom","tokens":["o191140020021","o191140020041"],"choice":"Judah ... Israel: the whole people","alternatives":[],"reason":"In the parallel lines Judah and Israel together stand for all the tribes.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"supplied_words","tokens":["o191140020021"],"choice":"God made","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable names God, to whom \"his\" refers (m1).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.3.s1","verses":[3],"source":"הַיָּ֣ם רָ֭אָה וַיָּנֹ֑ס הַ֝יַּרְדֵּ֗ן יִסֹּ֥ב לְאָחֽוֹר׃","gloss":"The sea saw and fled; the Jordan turned back.","pieces":[[3,"The sea looked and ran away; the Jordan turned and flowed back.",null]],"L":{"3":"The sea saw and fled; the Jordan turned back."},"R":{"3":"The sea saw it and ran away. The Jordan River stopped and went backward."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"allusion","tokens":["o191140030012","o191140030042"],"choice":"the sea and the Jordan: the two crossings","alternatives":[],"reason":"At the start of the journey the sea split for Israel (Exodus 14:21-22); at the end the Jordan stopped flowing so they could cross into the land (Joshua 3:13-17).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"idiom","tokens":["o191140030021","o191140030032"],"choice":"the sea saw and fled: creation in awe of God","alternatives":[],"reason":"The sea and river are pictured as living things that saw something and ran. What they saw is answered in verse 7: the presence of the Lord (77:17).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.4.s1","verses":[4],"source":"הֶֽ֭הָרִים רָקְד֣וּ כְאֵילִ֑ים גְּ֝בָע֗וֹת כִּבְנֵי־צֹֽאן׃","gloss":"The mountains skipped like rams, hills like young of [the] flock.","pieces":[[4,"The mountains sprang about like rams, the hills like lambs from the flock.",null]],"L":{"4":"The mountains skipped like rams, [the] hills like the young of the flock."},"R":{"4":"The mountains jumped around like rams. The hills jumped like little lambs."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"allusion","tokens":["o191140040012","o191140040021"],"choice":"the mountains skipped: Sinai shaking","alternatives":[],"reason":"When the LORD came down on Mount Sinai, the whole mountain shook (Exodus 19:18; Judges 5:5; 68:9). The poem pictures the shaking as rams and lambs leaping.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.5.s1","verses":[5],"source":"מַה־לְּךָ֣ הַ֭יָּם כִּ֣י תָנ֑וּס הַ֝יַּרְדֵּ֗ן תִּסֹּ֥ב לְאָחֽוֹר׃","gloss":"What [is] to you, sea, that you flee, Jordan, [that] you turn back,","pieces":[[5,"What is wrong, sea, that you run away? Jordan, why do you turn back?",null]],"L":{"5":"What [is it] with you, sea, that you flee, Jordan, [that] you turn back,"},"R":{"5":"Sea, why do you run away? Jordan River, why do you turn back?"},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140050011","o191140050051","o191140050071"],"choice":"What is it with you: a teasing question","alternatives":[],"reason":"The poet speaks to the sea, river, mountains and hills as if watching it happen now and asks what has frightened them. The answer comes in verse 7.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140050082"],"choice":"a sentence that runs on","alternatives":[],"reason":"At Literal the question continues into verse 6, so this verse ends with a comma. Balanced and Readable ask it as separate questions.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.6.s1","verses":[6],"source":"הֶֽ֭הָרִים תִּרְקְד֣וּ כְאֵילִ֑ים גְּ֝בָע֗וֹת כִּבְנֵי־צֹֽאן׃","gloss":"mountains, [that] you skip like rams, hills, like young of [the] flock?","pieces":[[6,"Mountains, why do you spring about like rams, and you hills, like lambs from the flock?",null]],"L":{"6":"mountains, [that] you skip like rams, hills, like the young of the flock?"},"R":{"6":"Mountains, why do you jump around like rams? Hills, why do you jump like little lambs?"},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140060021"],"choice":"the question repeats verse 4","alternatives":[],"reason":"Verses 5-6 repeat verses 3-4 as questions, which builds up to the answer of verse 7.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.7.s1","verses":[7],"source":"מִלִּפְנֵ֣י אָ֭דוֹן ח֣וּלִי אָ֑רֶץ מִ֝לִּפְנֵ֗י אֱל֣וֹהַּ יַעֲקֹֽב׃","gloss":"Before [the] Lord tremble, earth, before [the] God of Jacob,","pieces":[[7,"Earth, shake with fear before the Lord, before the God of Jacob,",null]],"L":{"7":"Before the Lord tremble, earth, before the God of Jacob,"},"R":{"7":"Earth, tremble when the Lord comes near! Tremble before the God of Jacob."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191140070021"],"choice":"the Lord: the Master","alternatives":[],"reason":"The word ('adon) is \"lord, master\". Here, with no other name, it is God, the Lord of all the earth (97:5). The psalm does not use the divine name at all.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191140070031"],"choice":"tremble: shake, writhe","alternatives":["dance"],"reason":"The verb (chul) is to shake or writhe, as in the pains of birth (29:8; 77:17). The answer to the questions is a command: all the earth should tremble as the sea and mountains did.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o191140070061"],"choice":"the God of Jacob: Eloah","alternatives":[],"reason":"Here the singular word for God (Eloah) is used, which is rare in the Psalms (18:32). \"Jacob\" names Israel by its ancestor, as in verse 1.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m4","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140070071"],"choice":"a sentence that runs on","alternatives":[],"reason":"The sentence continues into verse 8, so this verse ends with a comma at Literal and Balanced.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"PSA.114.8.s1","verses":[8],"source":"הַהֹפְכִ֣י הַצּ֣וּר אֲגַם־מָ֑יִם חַ֝לָּמִ֗ישׁ לְמַעְיְנוֹ־מָֽיִם׃","gloss":"who turns the rock [into] a pool of water, flint into a spring of water.","pieces":[[8,"who changed the rock into a pool of water, and hard flint into a flowing spring.",null]],"L":{"8":"who turns the rock [into] a pool of water, flint into a spring of water."},"R":{"8":"He turned solid rock into a pool of water. He made a spring of water flow out of hard stone."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"allusion","tokens":["o191140080022","o191140080031","o191140080051"],"choice":"water from the rock in the wilderness","alternatives":[],"reason":"The LORD gave Israel water from the rock at Horeb and at Meribah (Exodus 17:6; Numbers 20:11; Deuteronomy 8:15, \"water from the flint rock\"; 78:15-16).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o191140080012"],"choice":"who turns: what God does","alternatives":[],"reason":"The participle (hahofkhi) describes God by what he does. The earth trembles at the God who can make hard rock run with water.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"}],"words":{"1":[{"id":"o191140010011","text":"בְּ","after":"","lemma":"בְּ","morph":"R","gloss":"when","strong":"0871a","transliteration":"bə","lang":"H","parse":"Preposition"},{"id":"o191140010012","text":"צֵ֣את","after":" ","lemma":"יָצָא","morph":"Vqc","gloss":"went.out","strong":"3318","transliteration":"ṣēʾṯ","lang":"H","parse":"Verb · Qal · infinitive construct"},{"id":"o191140010021","text":"יִ֭שְׂרָאֵל","after":" ","lemma":"יִשְׂרָאֵל","morph":"Np","gloss":"Israel","strong":"3478","transliteration":"yiśrāʾēl","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o191140010031","text":"מִ","after":"","lemma":"מִן","morph":"R","gloss":"from","stro